B2. THE PREDICTED DEFECT HAPPENED, ON THE FIRST RUN, AND THE MECHANISM CAUGHT IT

  run_sweep's refusal fired before it scanned anything: `credential_assignment`
  did not match the canary. The pattern opened with `\b(api[_-]?key|...)`, and
  the field name in this repository is OPENROUTER_API_KEY -- `\bAPI_KEY` never
  matches it, because the preceding underscore is a word character.

  Without the canary that sweep returns ZERO credential findings over 251 files
  and is reported as clean. It would have been correct about six patterns and
  blind to the one that matters most in a repository whose only real secret is
  an OpenRouter key.

  Same shape as 9b / 17 / 18 / 20. What is new is WHERE it hid: in the detector
  itself -- the last place a security review looks, because the detector is what
  everything else is trusted through.


B3. THE MIRROR-IMAGE DEFECT, WHICH COST MORE TIME

  The first full run returned ~600 telephone hits (every one a float such as
  0.5462... in reports/*.json, or an ISO date) and 314 handle hits (every one a
  @dataclass decorator or a BibTeX @article{). Nine hundred rows of noise is a
  table nobody reads, and an unread table is an unchecked table.

  Fixes, and both generalise:
    - telephone: 9-15 digits (E.164's own ceiling), no decimal point, no bracket.
      -> 27 hits, then 10, all DOI/ISSN runs.
    - handle: must not BEGIN its line. Decorators and BibTeX entries always do;
      a handle in prose never does. Distinguishing by POSITION rather than by a
      list of decorator names is the difference between a detector that stays
      correct and one that needs editing every time a new library is imported.
      -> 8 hits, all in de-ID fixtures and prose about handles.


